49
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:44,020
So what exactly is a kernel?

50
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:48,470
The kernel is the core computer program
inside an operating system, and it acts

51
00:02:48,470 --> 00:02:51,990
as a direct bridge between your computer
software and its physical hardware.

52
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:56,320
So broadly speaking, it provides the
inter-infrastructure for applications,

53
00:02:56,380 --> 00:02:58,760
the user mode programs that we all write.

54
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,480
And you don't need to know it deeply,
but I just wanted to share the

55
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:03,400
definition so we're all on the same page.

56
00:03:04,290 --> 00:03:07,910
And the vast majority of software
engineers write application code like

57
00:03:07,910 --> 00:03:12,280
web apps, mobile apps, et cetera, and
that lives entirely inside user mode.

58
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:15,660
Only special engineers, like
operating system developers

59
00:03:15,710 --> 00:03:19,010
or device driver developers,
actually will work in kernel mode.

60
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:24,190
And if I write a user mode
application code, that code uses

61
00:03:24,190 --> 00:03:25,810
a lot of things in the kernel.

62
00:03:25,810 --> 00:03:30,890
It uses CPU runtime, it uses memory, all
kinds of devices, reading or writing over

63
00:03:30,890 --> 00:03:33,190
the network, reading a disk, a lot more.

64
00:03:33,690 --> 00:03:36,440
So the main difference
between the two is authority.

65
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:40,210
Kernel mode has unrestricted access
to the entire system hardware and

66
00:03:40,210 --> 00:03:44,870
memory, while user mode operates with
restricted privileges to protect the OS

67
00:03:44,870 --> 00:03:47,380
from cratchets… crashes, excuse me.

68
00:03:49,010 --> 00:03:49,880
So what is this?

69
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:52,110
Why am I showing JavaScript code here?

70
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:56,989
I thought when we were talking about
the kernel, so what's the point?

71
00:03:57,049 --> 00:04:00,830
It's because teaching through comparison
is, A, a good way to learn, and so

