00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:04,750
what we can see here is that we have
a button defined in HTML at the top,

73
00:04:05,110 --> 00:04:07,989
and below we have an event listener
in JavaScript that defines what

74
00:04:08,019 --> 00:04:09,470
happens when you click the button.

75
00:04:10,059 --> 00:04:11,150
So it's pretty simple.

76
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:14,869
There's a static HTML code and
a definition of what happens,

77
00:04:15,349 --> 00:04:18,559
and then you might ask, "How
do I handle a certain event?"

78
00:04:19,479 --> 00:04:24,350
Now, in parallel to eBPF, in eBPF,
we define the same sort of event

79
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:25,790
listener, but it's on the kernel.

80
00:04:25,790 --> 00:04:30,000
So the function at the top, TCP
receiving message, is a kernel

81
00:04:30,179 --> 00:04:35,200
function that's called every time
a packet arrives and before it

82
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:37,059
reaches our process in user mode.

83
00:04:37,239 --> 00:04:40,659
And below it, I have an eBPF
event listener that every time

84
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:44,129
a packet arrives, prints the ID
of the process that received it.

85
00:04:44,459 --> 00:04:46,629
So hopefully you're with me so far.

86
00:04:47,390 --> 00:04:49,400
So back to this comparison.

87
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:53,999
People like to say that eBPF is to
the kernel what JavaScript is to HTML.

88
00:04:54,479 --> 00:04:58,980
It's a well-known and common phrase and
we can credit Brendan Gregg here, but it's

89
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:04,329
said to appear in a lot of places and from
a lot of people as well So I wanna talk a

90
00:05:04,329 --> 00:05:08,129
little bit about the history as well, and
I really recommend reading the following.

91
00:05:08,129 --> 00:05:12,639
It's called eBPF Documentary: eBPF's
Creation Story and Unlocking the Kernel.

92
00:05:13,049 --> 00:05:17,689
It's an account by Thomas Graf,
who's the CTO at Cisco about the

93
00:05:17,689 --> 00:05:21,579
story of eBPF which originally
started around two thousand eleven.

94
00:05:21,989 --> 00:05:27,059
And basically, eBPF essentially
adds programmability to the kernel,

95
00:05:27,099 --> 00:05:28,579
and it makes the kernel dynamic.

96
00:05:28,919 --> 00:05:33,069
If you try to imagine HTML without
JavaScript, you get a page with some

97
00:05:33,459 --> 00:05:37,580
static thing in it that doesn't respond
at all to how we behave, and that's

98
00:05:37,629 --> 00:05:40,040
the power that eBPF adds to the kernel.

99
00:05:41,469 --> 00:05:45,059
And more and more uses keep appearing that
take advantage of this programmability,

100
00:05:45,539 --> 00:05:49,180
just like when JavaScript started
and peoples began to understand what

101
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:50,639
the Internet would look like today.

102
00:05:51,439 --> 00:05:53,189
So let's talk about how it all began.

103
00:05:53,219 --> 00:05:59,729
So first we had the world of before
eBPF rather, and in user spaces

104
00:05:59,769 --> 00:06:00,999
changing behavior would be cheap.

105
00:06:01,019 --> 00:06:04,159
You could edit, you could deploy,
you… and then it's live, and if

106
00:06:04,159 --> 00:06:08,079
it crashes and your process dies,
then something else restarts it.

107
00:06:08,689 --> 00:06:11,710
But the kernel works nothing like
that, and so you had two options.

108
00:06:11,729 --> 00:06:14,329
You had to either get a patch
merged upstream or ship an

109
00:06:14,339 --> 00:06:16,329
out-of-tree kernel module.

110
00:06:16,329 --> 00:06:20,349
The upstream option meant review cycles
and a kernel release distro picking it

111
00:06:20,349 --> 00:06:24,999
up, users upgrading, et cetera, and that
was like two to four years before that

112
00:06:24,999 --> 00:06:27,099
change would actually run on your machine.

113
00:06:27,719 --> 00:06:31,789
And then kernel modules ran immediately
with no isolation at all, and you have

114
00:06:31,789 --> 00:06:34,449
full privileges, no process boundaries.

115
00:06:34,929 --> 00:06:39,159
However, a bad pointer panics the
whole machine, not just your service.

116
00:06:39,639 --> 00:06:44,489
So from the perspective of anyone
building it or on it the kernel was frozen

117
00:06:45,981 --> 00:06:51,131
But now some pressure made this, this
conflict kind of untenable around 2011.

118
00:06:51,851 --> 00:06:55,401
And that's really when data centers
vis-virtualized, and everything

119
00:06:55,401 --> 00:06:56,511
started moving to the cloud.

120
00:06:56,521 --> 00:07:00,601
And so we encountered this bottleneck,
and the bottleneck was that there was

121
00:07:00,621 --> 00:07:05,691
application code that we as developers
were writing was no longer actually

122
00:07:05,691 --> 00:07:10,291
a bottleneck because I have tons of
applications running on the same server.

123
00:07:10,291 --> 00:07:12,880
And instead, the issue becomes
the ingress and egress of the

124
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:13,327
serter-server or the egress and ingress

125
00:07:14,327 --> 00:07:18,077
of the data center, and that's something
that suddenly had to be dealt with.

126
00:07:18,087 --> 00:07:21,947
Physical hosts stopped running
apps and started running packets

127
00:07:21,957 --> 00:07:26,337
between fleets of VMs, and software
networking slack-stack, and the kernel

128
00:07:26,347 --> 00:07:29,037
became load-bearing infrastructure.

129
00:07:29,777 --> 00:07:32,177
Meanwhile, everyone else was
running Linux in production

130
00:07:32,177 --> 00:07:33,647
and wanted it to stop changing.

131
00:07:33,647 --> 00:07:37,267
So we have this issue, just to
highlight this conflict between

132
00:07:37,267 --> 00:07:42,007
stability and velocity, and the
kernel really had no way to give both.

133
00:07:42,517 --> 00:07:49,867
And so this guy, Alexey known today
as the father of eBPF, comes along,

134
00:07:49,867 --> 00:07:51,257
and he s-kind of says the following.

135
00:07:51,257 --> 00:07:56,147
He says, "Load balancing and packet
filtering and all these logics that

136
00:07:56,147 --> 00:08:00,357
run on incoming and outgoing packets,
which are now the bottleneck, they need

137
00:08:00,357 --> 00:08:02,167
to actually run inside the kernel."

138
00:08:02,297 --> 00:08:03,017
And why?

139
00:08:03,567 --> 00:08:08,187
Because previously, these logics ran
from user mode, and packets that came in

140
00:08:08,227 --> 00:08:14,517
would go out to user mode, and something
decided what to do with them, that

141
00:08:14,517 --> 00:08:17,837
they'd come back to the kernel, and then
they could be received by the process

142
00:08:17,837 --> 00:08:19,347
that was supposed to receive them.

143
00:08:19,787 --> 00:08:23,047
And so if we move them to the
kernel, then we'll save a lot.

144
00:08:24,857 --> 00:08:30,057
So yeah, he basically says, "Let's
write a new instruction set." And the

145
00:08:30,057 --> 00:08:33,457
instruction set, for those who are
unfamiliar, is essentially code for

146
00:08:33,457 --> 00:08:35,367
how to talk to the process-processor.

147
00:08:35,367 --> 00:08:38,127
It's like Intel's x86
or Arm or many others.

148
00:08:38,597 --> 00:08:41,997
And he says, "Let's write a new one,
but not actually build a new processor.

149
00:08:41,997 --> 00:08:45,107
Instead, let's write this code, and
the kernel will execute it virtually."

150
00:08:45,557 --> 00:08:49,547
And the reason why this route gets
interesting is because it started

151
00:08:49,547 --> 00:08:53,187
with a networking incident that led
him to an uncomfortable conclusion,

152
00:08:53,187 --> 00:08:58,707
which is that you can't fully trust
the compiler to admit what you meant.

153
00:08:59,167 --> 00:09:02,287
And so you don't trust the code at all.

154
00:09:02,367 --> 00:09:07,017
So before the kernel loads a program,
it has to be written in this instruction

155
00:09:07,017 --> 00:09:12,407
set and walk every possible path
through it and prove that the thing

156
00:09:12,417 --> 00:09:15,817
will terminate and only touches the
memory that it was actually given.

157
00:09:16,177 --> 00:09:19,047
And if it fails the proof, then
the program doesn't actually load.

158
00:09:19,317 --> 00:09:23,487
And then you pass it an unrestricted
code and safely run in the kernel space.

159
00:09:23,537 --> 00:09:24,777
And so that's the whole trick.

160
00:09:24,777 --> 00:09:27,667
You're converting this into a proof.

161
00:09:28,667 --> 00:09:33,207
And so then Daniel Borkman comes on
the scene and he's a developer with

162
00:09:33,207 --> 00:09:35,897
experience with writing kernel code,
and he gets excited about the j-

163
00:09:36,087 --> 00:09:39,047
idea, and together they develop eBPF.

164
00:09:39,057 --> 00:09:43,767
And they call it eBPF because it's
a technology in the kernel and

165
00:09:43,847 --> 00:09:48,197
previously there was already BPF,
which is just without the extended bit.

166
00:09:48,657 --> 00:09:53,527
And they call it eBPF because of
the similarity, and the need also

167
00:09:53,527 --> 00:09:55,237
came from somewhat different places.

168
00:09:55,247 --> 00:10:01,507
They weren't just thinking about BPF,
BB- BPP- BPF at first, and BPF is also

169
00:10:01,517 --> 00:10:05,127
a specific machine that's not nearly
powerful enough to do what they needed.

170
00:10:05,577 --> 00:10:07,027
But that's where the name comes from

171
00:10:08,027 --> 00:10:13,567
And once it all shipped, the economics
flipped and it starts becoming effective.

172
00:10:13,897 --> 00:10:17,557
So you can compile a program, load it
into a running machine, and it's live.

173
00:10:17,557 --> 00:10:20,917
And so what used to take years
now take just a few days.

174
00:10:21,367 --> 00:10:26,287
And the first proof of scale was an
L4 load balancer from Facebook and

175
00:10:26,287 --> 00:10:31,357
roughly fifty nanoseconds per packet
with ten times faster than IPVS.

176
00:10:31,717 --> 00:10:35,037
And so that project was known as
Katran, and they open source it.

177
00:10:35,437 --> 00:10:36,427
But there is a catch.

178
00:10:36,477 --> 00:10:41,067
Writing eBPF at this point means
writing byte code and then arguing

179
00:10:41,257 --> 00:10:44,077
with a verifier that rejects you
for reasons you don't understand.

180
00:10:44,077 --> 00:10:48,157
So huge black box and realistically,
you really needed a kernel team.

181
00:10:48,157 --> 00:10:51,747
And I just wanna keep this in mind because
I don't want one question you to be

182
00:10:51,767 --> 00:10:56,127
asking is like, "Why are you talking to
me about a technology that is so old?"

183
00:10:56,137 --> 00:11:00,187
Because the reality is it took quite a
while for things to catch up, to mature

184
00:11:00,187 --> 00:11:03,547
to the point where they are today, where
a bunch of cool companies can be doing

185
00:11:03,567 --> 00:11:05,777
things that we see happening today.

186
00:11:06,777 --> 00:11:12,097
And so in 2015 Brendan Gregg,
whose quote we saw earlier and

187
00:11:12,097 --> 00:11:16,107
others also start realize that
there are a ton of uses for eBPF.

188
00:11:16,907 --> 00:11:19,697
It came from the networking
world that we talked about.

189
00:11:19,997 --> 00:11:23,837
But Brendan Gregg is also a performance
guy, and he was working on observability

190
00:11:23,837 --> 00:11:25,777
at Net- Netflix at the time.

191
00:11:26,477 --> 00:11:29,687
And he realized that with this
programmability at the kernel, you

192
00:11:29,687 --> 00:11:31,317
can also do a lot of other things.

193
00:11:31,747 --> 00:11:36,547
So he leads the development of
platforms like bcc and bpftrace

194
00:11:36,617 --> 00:11:37,757
which we will see later.

195
00:11:38,077 --> 00:11:42,407
And it also made it easy to
write eBPF code and come with a

196
00:11:42,407 --> 00:11:46,227
ton of ready-made eBPF tools for
anyone who wants to use them.

197
00:11:46,827 --> 00:11:50,897
So I really like this comic quite
a bit just because it explains

