00:17:03,777 --> 00:17:05,907
eBPF probes for this example

295
00:17:06,907 --> 00:17:10,457
So there's a kernel function called
do_nanosleep that's called every

296
00:17:10,457 --> 00:17:13,427
time a program tries to sleep,
and that's an interesting place.

297
00:17:13,847 --> 00:17:15,127
And my probe is pretty simple.

298
00:17:15,157 --> 00:17:19,977
Every time there's a do_nanosleep, it
writes which processes went to sleep, and

299
00:17:19,977 --> 00:17:26,307
it uses a BPF helper, and helpers are the
functions that can call from eBPF co-code.

300
00:17:26,577 --> 00:17:29,087
And this helper is called
bpf_get_current_pid_tgid

301
00:17:30,957 --> 00:17:33,347
to get the process ID.

302
00:17:34,877 --> 00:17:40,117
And so then I compile this
thing to a target that is BPF,

303
00:17:40,667 --> 00:17:42,517
and that's the instruction set.

304
00:17:42,537 --> 00:17:46,387
And I don't compile it to x86,
even though in the example we're

305
00:17:46,387 --> 00:17:48,627
discussing it'll run on that processor.

306
00:17:48,847 --> 00:17:50,597
Instead, I compile it to BPF.

307
00:17:50,637 --> 00:17:51,847
And what does that look like?

308
00:17:52,217 --> 00:17:56,237
My helper has a number 14, and
every helper has a number, and

309
00:17:56,237 --> 00:18:00,587
every instruction I compile into
a byte sequence into byte code.

310
00:18:00,607 --> 00:18:04,807
And this is what BPF byte code
looks like, and this is the object

311
00:18:04,807 --> 00:18:08,577
file that I compiled, for example.

312
00:18:09,577 --> 00:18:15,777
A jump to BPF helper is an instruction
whose sequence number is zero eighty-five,

313
00:18:15,797 --> 00:18:17,487
and my helper number is fourteen.

314
00:18:17,757 --> 00:18:21,217
So right inside the code, we can
see a byte sequence of zero by

315
00:18:21,277 --> 00:18:23,717
eighty-five, some zeros, and fourteen.

316
00:18:23,787 --> 00:18:26,067
And essentially all my
code is in this byte code.

317
00:18:26,387 --> 00:18:29,407
And this code is going to run inside
the kernel, which is pretty dangerous

318
00:18:29,407 --> 00:18:32,257
if you stop and think about it for
a second, because what I'm telling

319
00:18:32,257 --> 00:18:35,277
you is that from a user mode I
can write whatever code I want and

320
00:18:35,277 --> 00:18:36,607
it's gonna get stuck in the kernel.

321
00:18:36,887 --> 00:18:40,817
And the kernel did a ton of work to secure
this and make sure it didn't get stuck.

322
00:18:42,087 --> 00:18:46,267
So there's a kernel component called
the verifier that goes over the eBPF

323
00:18:46,317 --> 00:18:49,367
code and makes sure it doesn't do
anything we don't want it to do.

324
00:18:49,637 --> 00:18:52,227
So its job is to impose
all kinds of restrictions.

325
00:18:52,537 --> 00:18:55,447
It limits the amount of
memory code my code can use.

326
00:18:55,657 --> 00:18:58,907
It limits the number of instructions
that I can run, so I don't run an

327
00:18:58,917 --> 00:19:01,957
infinite loop and hang the kernel.

328
00:19:01,957 --> 00:19:05,487
It doesn't allow my kernel to sleep,
plus many other restrictions as well.

329
00:19:05,777 --> 00:19:09,947
So to verify all these things, it goes
over my code and keeps a ton of states,

330
00:19:09,957 --> 00:19:11,917
and it walks every flow in the code.

331
00:19:12,217 --> 00:19:15,777
So say I have some variable in the
code called X, and in my code I

332
00:19:15,777 --> 00:19:17,547
check if X is greater than three.

333
00:19:17,857 --> 00:19:21,687
So now I've just created a branch,
and the kernel verifier walks this

334
00:19:21,687 --> 00:19:25,067
flow on one hand if X is greater than
three, and on the other hand if it's

335
00:19:25,067 --> 00:19:28,407
less than or equal to three, and it
continues down both flows, running

336
00:19:28,407 --> 00:19:29,887
like that all the way to the end.

337
00:19:30,107 --> 00:19:33,307
And then it verifies that in
every flow my code exists, that

338
00:19:33,307 --> 00:19:35,107
it doesn't do anything stupid.

339
00:19:35,547 --> 00:19:39,947
So in this way, the verifier is really the
part that creates the trust, and it's what

340
00:19:39,997 --> 00:19:44,507
converts the question from, "Do we trust
this code?" to, "We trust this proof."

341
00:19:45,717 --> 00:19:50,757
So now that the kernel has this code and
that it is approved for execution and it

