00:26:53,257 --> 00:26:58,057
But there is a problem with that,
and the problem is that it just will

445
00:26:58,067 --> 00:27:02,747
print me everything written to the
file, and I wanna know which file.

446
00:27:03,747 --> 00:27:09,517
So the write syscall doesn't get you a
file name, it gets a file descriptor,

447
00:27:09,577 --> 00:27:12,627
and that's exactly how you work with it.

448
00:27:13,347 --> 00:27:17,617
And to get the file descriptor, you first
have to open the file, so I actually

449
00:27:17,617 --> 00:27:25,397
need to put another probe on open which
we've already seen, and I will… Right

450
00:27:25,397 --> 00:27:29,607
here we have seen, and I will then
filter this probe to run only when

451
00:27:29,607 --> 00:27:35,647
my argument is the file name matching
the actual file that I care about.

452
00:27:36,037 --> 00:27:39,347
And I'll save that particular
invocation of open.

453
00:27:39,647 --> 00:27:40,657
And why will I do this?

454
00:27:40,697 --> 00:27:44,077
Because on the way out of open,
I get the file descriptor.

455
00:27:44,087 --> 00:27:50,247
So I need a third probe, okay, so that I
can save it into a map in kernel memory

456
00:27:50,667 --> 00:27:53,977
that this invocation of open interests me.

457
00:27:54,337 --> 00:27:58,687
And then on the exit from open,
if it was an open on a file that

458
00:27:58,687 --> 00:28:03,397
I care about, I'm gonna save the
file descriptor in a second map.

459
00:28:04,007 --> 00:28:10,807
So I have the FDS map, which maps
from the process ID, To the relevant

460
00:28:10,807 --> 00:28:15,607
file descriptor that now corresponds
only to the file I care about.

461
00:28:15,937 --> 00:28:19,907
And I'll filter the probe I already
wrote on the write syscall to

462
00:28:19,907 --> 00:28:24,857
fire only when the file descriptor
matches the file that I care about.

463
00:28:25,167 --> 00:28:28,347
And when I run this thing
the whole flow will happen.

464
00:28:28,627 --> 00:28:30,707
We'll say a process writes to the file.

465
00:28:30,757 --> 00:28:35,727
It will then call open, and
it'll get a file descriptor.

466
00:28:35,747 --> 00:28:40,277
You track all of that, and the moment
it writes to the file, my probe on write

467
00:28:40,287 --> 00:28:43,067
sees it and prints the actual data for us.

468
00:28:44,177 --> 00:28:49,327
So here we attach three probes at this
time, and is a little bit more complicated

469
00:28:49,427 --> 00:28:52,787
with state between them, but the probes
really communicate with each other, and

470
00:28:52,787 --> 00:28:55,087
the moment I write to the file, we see it

